// HARNESS: SKIP — KNOWN FAIL (BUG#61, native backend). Tracks a crash.
//
// Calling a stdlib method (is_some/unwrap/...) on an Option returned by a GENERIC
// function segfaults on the native backend. A NON-generic function's Option works
// (see below), and `match` on the SAME generic-returned Option works, so the bug
// is specific to the stdlib method-dispatch/self-passing path when the Option came
// from a monomorphised generic. This is why `Vec[T].get(i).unwrap()` (get[T] is
// generic, returns Option[T]) crashes. PRE-EXISTING (the pre-BUG#57 compiler also
// crashes — not caused by the Option/Result match work). The C backend is correct.
//
// Minimal isolation (all native):
//   fn getopt(x: i32) -> Option[i32]: return Some(x)   // NON-generic
//   let o = getopt(7); o.unwrap()                      // => 7  (works)
//   fn myget[T](x: T) -> Option[T]: return Some(x)     // GENERIC
//   let o = myget(7); o.unwrap()                       // => CRASH
//   let o = myget(7); o.is_some()                      // => CRASH
//   match myget(7) { Some(v): .. }                     // => 7  (works; my match dispatch)
//   let o = myget(7); <use o without a method>         // => works
//
// So: the generic getter's box is fine (match reads it); only the stdlib method
// path breaks on it. AIR for the caller is byte-identical to the non-generic case;
// the divergence is the box's Option type-id (generic-inner vs concrete i32-inner),
// suggesting the payload type isn't fully monomorphised on the Option the method
// receives. Needs a dedicated generic-monomorphisation + method-dispatch pass
// (dump-air + objdump), NOT a guess. Remove SKIP when fixed.
fn myget[T](x: T) -> Option[T]:
    return Some(x)

fn main() -> i32:
    let o = myget(7)
    return o.unwrap()
